ALGORITHMS RE-IMPLEMENTED

tiny-skia — https://github.com/linebender/tiny-skia
  BSD-3-Clause. tiny-skia is a port of Skia. The anti-aliased scan converter, hairline stroking, cubic and quadratic
  subdivision counts, dash-splitting rules, lowp blend arithmetic, f32
  layer-compositing pipeline and gradient evaluation in
  LeanSvg/{Raster,Geom,Canvas,Shader}.lean are fixed-point Lean
  re-implementations of its algorithms. No Rust or C++ source is included.
  Licence text: licenses/code/tiny-skia-LICENSE.txt, from
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/linebender/tiny-skia/
  5d4754777746eef0828be166896eaf482c49f8f2/LICENSE.

HarfBuzz / harfrust — https://github.com/harfbuzz/harfrust
  MIT. The OpenType layout engine, normaliser and Arabic/Hebrew/Indic
  shapers in LeanSvg/{Shape,ShapeRun}.lean are Lean re-implementations of
  harfrust 0.12.0's algorithms (itself a port of HarfBuzz). No Rust or C++
  source is included. Licence text: licenses/code/harfrust-LICENSE.txt.

unicode-bidi — https://github.com/servo/unicode-bidi
  MIT OR Apache-2.0. LeanSvg/Bidi.lean re-implements its UAX #9 algorithm.
  Licence text (MIT option): licenses/code/unicode-bidi-LICENSE-MIT.txt.

matplotlib — https://github.com/matplotlib/matplotlib
  Matplotlib License (PSF-based, BSD-compatible). LeanSvg/StixNonUnicodeTable.lean
  is generated (tests/gen_stix_nonunicode.py) from matplotlib v3.9.2's
  `stix_virtual_fonts` table in lib/matplotlib/_mathtext_data.py: which
  private-use code point of the STIX 1.x "STIXNonUnicode" font stands for which
  letter. Licence text: licenses/code/matplotlib-LICENSE.txt, from
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/matplotlib/matplotlib/v3.9.2/LICENSE/LICENSE.

Brotli — RFC 7932 and https://github.com/google/brotli
  MIT. LeanSvg/Brotli.lean is a Lean decoder written from RFC 7932, checked
  against the brotli 1.2.0 C decoder's tables. No C source is included.
  Licence text: licenses/code/brotli-LICENSE.txt.

WOFF 2.0 — https://www.w3.org/TR/WOFF2/ and fontTools
  (https://github.com/fonttools/fonttools, MIT). The glyf/loca and hmtx
  reconstruction in LeanSvg/Woff.lean follows the W3C specification;
  fontTools' woff2 module was the reference for the triplet decoding. No
  Python source is included.

